  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`. It typechecks, but I could not run `vite build` or open the scene in a browser, so none of the visuals have been checked.\n\n- **Island:** a flat-shaded polar-grid terrain with per-face colours (seabed, sand, grass, rock, snow cap). About 20 low-poly pine trees and 12 rocks sit on the slopes. Trees and rocks are one flat-shaded mesh, with no model files.\n- **Water:** a custom GLSL surface with animated waves, and facets that come from screen-space derivatives (`dFdx`/`dFdy`). It has fresnel reflection, sun and moon glints, whitecaps, and animated foam stripes along the shore. It is slightly translucent and fades to the horizon colour with distance.\n- **Day/night:** one cycle takes 20 seconds. A Preetham sky with stars gets the sun position every second frame. The sun and moon directional lights, the hemisphere light, and the water colours all follow the same clock. The sun and moon lights are hidden when their intensity is near zero. At night the moon light, hemisphere light and a faint neutral environment keep the scene from going black.\n- **Camera:** an orbit camera at 8 m height and 24 m distance, rotating slowly. I computed that the whole island fits in frame and that some sky shows above the horizon.\n- **Contract:** it draws into `#app` with `resize: false`, renders every frame, and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__` after the first frame. I avoided top-level `await` because there is no Vite config and esbuild's default build target would reject it.\n\nLight intensities and vertex-colour brightness are my best guesses. Check them in a capture, along with whether the water looks right against the sky at the horizon.\n\n",
